<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Busby</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1802">1802</a>–<a href="/years/1871">1871</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Busby was a Scottish-born British official who served as the first British Resident in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> from 1833 to 1840. With little real power, he encouraged northern Māori chiefs to adopt a national flag in 1834 and to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-independence-of-new-zealand">Declaration of the Independence of New Zealand</a> at Waitangi in 1835. He helped draft the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a> of 1840, signed in the grounds of his house, and is also remembered as a founder of the Australian wine industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-busby.jpg" alt="James Busby" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: McDonald, James Ingram, 1865-1935 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JamesBusbyNZ.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Busby was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, the son of the English engineer John Busby and Sarah Kennedy, and emigrated with his parents to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> in 1824. Under a policy that encouraged free settlers with capital, he received a grant of 2,000 acres in the Coal River area of the Hunter Valley and began growing grapes, while running the farm and teaching viticulture at the Male Orphans School near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>.</p><p>After losing that post in 1827 and serving as head tax collector until 1829, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1831 to petition the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a> and toured the vineyards of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, bringing back the first collection of vine stock to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. A report on the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> won him the post of British Resident in March 1832.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Busby married Agnes Dow in the Hunter Region on 1 November 1832 and reached the Bay of Islands on HMS Imogene on 5 May 1833. His duties, set by Governor Richard Bourke of New South Wales, were to protect British commerce and mediate between European settlers and Māori, but he was given no means to enforce his authority. The Treaty House built for him at Waitangi still stands, and wine was made there from his vines before those in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> bore fruit.</p><p>The flag was proposed after an unregistered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> ship was seized in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, and the Declaration of Independence answered the plan of Baron Charles de Thierry to claim French sovereignty. Tribal fighting in 1836 and 1837 led Busby to call his position untenable, and Bourke sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-hobson">William Hobson</a> to assess it. With Hobson he co-wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a>, first signed on 5 and 6 February 1840.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Busby declined a post in the colonial government and turned to farming, but debt and long litigation over his land titles followed; in 1868 he settled for scrip worth £36,800, which he sold for £23,000 after spending nearly £14,000 on legal fees. He sat on the Auckland Provincial Council from 1853 and edited the Aucklander from 1861 to 1863. He died at Anerley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 15 July 1871 and was buried at West Norwood Cemetery. Viscount Bledisloe bought the derelict Waitangi property in the 1930s and gave it to the New Zealand Government.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Busby” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The British Resident, <b>James Busby</b>, encouraged northern chiefs to choose a national flag in 1834 so that New Zealand-built ships could be registered.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-tribes-of-new-zealand">United Tribes of New Zealand</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Anerley, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-3.19&amp;year=1846" aria-label="Edinburgh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4481 258 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James Busby"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4931 528h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4931 528h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-3.19&amp;year=1846">What was here in 1846? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
